  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040587/assets/b07f18c13e3bca94d2b55d61b4a63b2a294d0e47ed8a2853f5e10c5140047b27.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(-3\\sqrt{3},-3),$ find the polar equation of the ray. Assume that the polar angle is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061|\\frac{-3}{-3\\sqrt{3}}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the third quadrant. So, we find the angle in the third quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$",
